module Core

# ============================================================================
# B64 -> B16 narrowing for the CORE-MATH binary16 ports
# ============================================================================
# The binary16 ports that compute in binary64 (erf, erfc, lgamma, tgamma, pow) round their result once,
# from binary64 straight to binary16. `B16(from: B64)` lowers to an LLVM `fptrunc double to half`, which
# on Windows x86-64 becomes a compiler-rt libcall (`__truncdfhf2`) whose return register does not match
# what LLVM expects, so it returns garbage there. This helper does the same single rounding through
# binary32 with round-to-odd, which needs only the double->float and float->half conversions (both exact
# hardware operations): binary32 keeps 13 more bits than binary16, so rounding to odd first and then to
# nearest gives exactly the correctly rounded (ties-to-even) binary16 result.

### Narrows a `B64` to `B16` with one round-to-nearest (ties to even), like a C `(_Float16)` cast of a
### double. NaN keeps its sign and top payload bits and becomes quiet, infinities stay infinite.
### :param d: The binary64 value.
### :returns: `d` rounded to the nearest `B16`.
secret routine b16_narrow_b64(d: B64) -> B16
  var f = B32(from: d)
  var fb = f.to_bits()
  var back = B64(from: f)
  # round to odd: when the binary32 rounding was inexact and landed on an even significand, step one
  # ulp back toward d (the truncated value, whose last bit is then odd)
  if not d.is_nan() and not f.is_infinite() and back != d and fb & 1_u32 == 0
    if back.abs() > d.abs()
      fb = fb -% 1
    else
      fb = fb +% 1
  return B16(from: B32.from_bits(bits: fb))
